<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best family ski trip is built around easy logistics. Choose a resort with a dedicated learning area, a strong ski school, slow beginner lifts, and lodging close to the base. Book kids' lessons early, keep everyone warm and fed, plan shorter ski days than you think, and set clear meeting points.</p></div>

<h2>How to pick a family-friendly resort</h2>
<p>The best family ski resorts aren't always the biggest or snowiest. Look for:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A dedicated learning area</strong> with gentle slopes, carpets or slow lifts, and separation from faster skiers.</li>
  <li><strong>A well-run ski school</strong> with programs for your kids' ages and full-day options.</li>
  <li><strong>Short transfers.</strong> A long drive from the airport is hard on young kids. Check the distance from the nearest airport on Snow Way's <%= link_to "resort pages by state", regions_path %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Slow, easy beginner lifts</strong> that kids can load and unload with confidence.</li>
  <li><strong>Lodging close to the base,</strong> so you aren't carrying gear and kids through parking lots. See <%= link_to "what ski-in/ski-out really means", guide_path("ski-in-ski-out") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Plenty of green and easy blue terrain</strong> so the family can ski together once kids progress.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Kids' ski school by age</h2>
<p>Every resort sets its own age ranges, but programs commonly look like this:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Toddlers (about 3 and under):</strong> childcare or snow play programs, with brief, playful time on skis if any.</li>
  <li><strong>Young kids (about 3 to 6):</strong> short, game-based lessons with breaks indoors. Many resorts start lessons around age 3 or 4.</li>
  <li><strong>School-age kids (about 6 to 12):</strong> group lessons that progress from the learning area to green and blue runs.</li>
  <li><strong>Teens:</strong> teen groups or adult lessons, depending on the resort.</li>
</ul>
<p>Book lessons as soon as you book lodging, especially for holiday weeks, which can sell out. Adults learning too can read <%= link_to "ski lessons for adults", guide_path("ski-lessons-for-adults") %>.</p>

<h2>Gear and rentals for kids</h2>
<p>Kids outgrow gear fast, so renting usually makes sense, especially when you fly. Many shops offer seasonal kids' rentals or trade-in programs if you ski often. Reserve rentals ahead and pick them up the evening before to avoid morning lines. Make sure boots fit snugly with one pair of thin ski socks, and that every child has a helmet that fits. The <%= link_to "ski size chart", ski_size_chart_path %> gives a quick length check, though shops will size kids' skis for you.</p>

<h2>Keeping kids warm and happy</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Dress in layers: a synthetic or wool base layer, a fleece, and waterproof jacket and pants or bibs. More in <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>.</li>
  <li>Mittens keep small hands warmer than gloves.</li>
  <li>Use a neck gaiter or balaclava and goggles on cold or windy days.</li>
  <li>Pack snacks and water in your pocket. Hungry, cold kids are done skiing.</li>
  <li>Bring hand warmers and a spare pair of dry gloves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A realistic family ski day</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Early breakfast</strong> and gear on at the lodging, not in the base area.</li>
  <li><strong>Morning ski school drop-off,</strong> giving adults a few runs on their own.</li>
  <li><strong>Lunch together,</strong> ideally early to beat the crowds.</li>
  <li><strong>A short afternoon session</strong> as a family on easy terrain.</li>
  <li><strong>Done by mid-afternoon,</strong> before kids are exhausted. Pools, sledding, or hot chocolate make a great finish.</li>
</ol>
<p>Plan for fewer hours on snow than you'd ski alone. A shorter, happy day beats a long, tearful one.</p>

<h2>First-day tips for kids</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Practice at home.</strong> Let kids walk around in their ski boots and try on helmets and goggles before the trip.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep expectations low.</strong> Day one is about having fun in the snow. Progress comes with time and lessons.</li>
  <li><strong>Let instructors teach.</strong> Kids often listen to an instructor more readily than to a parent, and lessons follow a proven progression.</li>
  <li><strong>Celebrate small wins:</strong> the first stop, the first chairlift ride, the first run from the top of the beginner lift.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch the weather.</strong> A mild, sunny day is ideal for a first day; very cold or stormy days are hard on new skiers. Check <%= link_to "where it's snowing this week", where_is_it_snowing_path %> to plan around big storms.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Lodging tips</h2>
<p>A condo or rental with a kitchen and washer can save money and make wet gear easier to manage. Being close to the lifts matters more for families than for any other group. Check whether the resort or lodging offers shuttles if you'll stay in town.</p>

<h2>Budget tips</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Avoid holiday weeks if your schedule allows; prices and crowds peak then.</li>
  <li>Look for multi-day ticket discounts and kids' ticket deals, which vary by resort.</li>
  <li>Compare pass options if you'll take more than one trip; see <%= link_to "Ikon vs Epic", ikon_vs_epic_path %>.</li>
  <li>Pack lunches or snacks; on-mountain food adds up fast for a family.</li>
  <li>Use the budget worksheet in the <%= link_to "free Ski Trip Planner", ski_trip_planner_path %>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Safety basics</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Helmets for everyone, every run.</li>
  <li>Pick a clear meeting point, like a lift base or lodge entrance, and agree on what to do if anyone gets separated.</li>
  <li>Put a card with your phone number in each child's jacket pocket.</li>
  <li>Teach kids to stop at the side of the run, not in the middle or below a blind spot.</li>
  <li>Stick to terrain everyone in the group can handle comfortably.</li>
</ul>
